```rust
/// Build the read-only exploration dispatcher (#881 slice 2).
///
/// This is the *composition* the previous slice shipped the halves for:
/// `READ_ONLY_CHILD_REMOVED_TOOLS` / `read_only_child_disallowed` (the pool)
/// and `sub_agent` (the dispatcher). Until this landed the only thing that
/// joined them was the process-global `--read-only-subagents` flag, so the
/// answer to "explore this, and you may not write" was *every sub-agent in the
/// session is read-only*, never *this dispatch is*. Here the narrowing is a
/// property of the TOOL, so a caller picks it per dispatch and the flag is
/// untouched.
///
/// Reuses, rather than re-spells, the three existing pieces: the fold
/// (`read_only_child_disallowed`), the child tool set
/// (`sub_agent_child_tools`) and the whole dispatch assembly
/// (`dispatch_tool_with_fallback`, which also gives this tool the same
/// fallback retry and the same diagnostic wrapper as `sub_agent`).
///
/// **Deliberately a leaf: the dispatched child gets no nested `sub_agent`.**
/// Giving it one would mean calling `build_sub_agent_tool_at_depth` from here,
/// whose nested tool builds ITS children from `config.disallowed_tools` — so
/// at depth 2 the write-class tools would come back, and "read-only by
/// construction" would be true of one hop only. A copy of the depth-walker
/// that threaded the read-only fold through every level was the alternative,
/// and the announced limit is cheaper and more honest than a silent one. The
/// description names this limit, and so does the write-up.
pub(crate) fn build_explore_agent_tool(
    config: &AgentConfig,
    shared_state: &SharedState,
) -> Box<dyn AgentTool> {
    // The ONE fold — never a second literal list of write-class names.
    let child_disallowed = read_only_child_disallowed(&config.disallowed_tools);
    let child_tools =
        sub_agent_child_tools(&config.dir_restrictions, &child_disallowed);
    dispatch_tool_with_fallback(config, &EXPLORE_AGENT_FLAVOR, child_tools, shared_state)
}
```

